{"title":"2022 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 10","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A dime is worth $0.10 and a quarter is worth $0.25. Terry has a jar that contains $1.00 worth of dimes and $1.00 worth of quarters. If he randomly removes one coin from the jar, what is the probability that it is a dime?\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/7/ce7429c2a633eaed4d0d4adbc9f5c3a18cb730a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\frac{1}{10} \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac27 \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{10}{11} \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{5}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"389\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> dimes in a dollar and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> quarters in a dollar, so Terry would have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> dimes and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> quarters. He would have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/6/dc603d18e0a61d171835631fcf960a065ca1d652.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10 + 4 = 14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"94\" height=\"15\" /> coins in the jar in total.\n</p><p>Removing one coin, we would have a probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/c/a0ce8cb4ef83e0525be8b91d314ffee836d70711.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{10}{14}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" /> of picking a dime, which simplifies to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/5/475bbe6637c25fd92f6d25c9b3da54668576f4e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(E) } \\frac{5}{7}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"60\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher \n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)\" title=\"2022 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)\">2022 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2022 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2022 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Pr